      "summary": "Full canonical overview of Our Kingdom Come: ancient pre-deluvial Zingara, the PC Triumvirate, the Ascendancy Trap, and Game Master sandbox directives.",
      "content": "<h3>1.1 High-Concept Campaign Premise</h3>\n<p>A Realm Shattered by Decadence and Eldritch Hubris. Set within the pre-deluvial, sword-and-sorcery epoch of ancient Zingara, Our Kingdom Come paints a dark portrait of a decadent, fallen nation rotting under the cumulative horrors of ancient sorcery, industrial necromantic exploitation, and cosmic ruin. Emerging from a complex ethnic synthesis—where pastoral Zingg Valley agriculturalists merged with conquering Pictish tribes and invading Hyborian warriors—Zingara stands as a land of sharp, irreconcilable contrasts. Flamboyant chivalric pageantry, touchy aristocratic honor, and soaring sea-facing bastions directly overlook rat-infested alleys, reeking industrial zombie pits, and subterranean abysses.</p>\n\n<div class=\"gm-callout\">\n  <div class=\"gm-badge\">GM CONFIDENTIAL &bull; THE ASCENDANCY TRAP</div>\n  <p>The Ascendancy Trap and Shadowed Puppetmasters. The primary narrative engine of the campaign centers on an operational crisis of political manipulation, bankruptcy, and decay: the Player Characters are the newly installed rulers—a Triumvirate of blood-related descendants of King Ancillus I—who have inherited a bankrupt, besieged kingdom lingering on the edge of utter collapse. Installed following a bloody civil war by a shadowy cabal of monarchist elites (the Restoration Council and the head of the royal army), the PCs' ascension is not a victory, but a calculated trap. Their handlers view the untested rulers as weak, inexperienced stand-ins meant to be easily manipulated, taking the public blame for empty coffers and social unrest while the Council governs from behind the throne.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Breaking the Leash in a Player-Led Sandbox. Despite their handlers' expectations, the PCs are capable, driven protagonists determined to break their puppetmasters' grip, assert true royal authority, and stabilize the realm. Trapped between internal handlers restricting royal resources and external threats—including republican insurgents in the subterranean ruins of The Pit, underground religious zealots, rogue zombie packs, and foreign invaders—the Player Characters must take direct, hands-on action. By securing ancient pre-Cataclysmic relics, navigating court intrigue, and executing strategic interventions across the fiefdoms, the Triumvirate must forge their own destiny and rebuild Zingara in a high-stakes, player-led sandbox campaign.</p>\n\n<h3>1.2 Canonical Worldbuilding Parameters</h3>\n<p>The following parameters strictly define the inviolable boundaries of the campaign setting strictly derived from the source context. No elements outside these parameters may be introduced.</p>\n<p>Allowed Setting Elements</p>\n<p>Excluded Setting Elements</p>\n<p>Elves: Ancient ocean-faring scholars who established magical traditions and founded the royal academy.</p>\n<p>Gunpowder: Cannon, flintlocks, matchlocks, or black powder weaponry of any kind.</p>\n<p>Cataclysm: Earthquakes, meteoric starfalls, and pre-deluvial geographical shifts.</p>\n<p>Modern+ Technologies: Steam power, combustion engines, electrical systems, or modern firearms.</p>\n<p>Zombies: Memory-retaining, enthralled undead utilized for hazardous industrial labor and military forces.</p>\n<p>Eldritch Sorcery: Dark blood rituals, cosmic deities, necromancy, and ancient sorcerous artifacts.</p>\n\n<h3>1.3 Game Master Execution & Sandbox Directives</h3>\n<p>Manage the Dual-Layer Operational Dynamic: Maintain constant tension between high-level courtly decisions (managing state debt, noble feuds, regional defense) and hands-on adventuring. The PCs cannot rely on subordinates to solve major crises because their royal guard and advisors are loyal to the Restoration Council.</p>\n<p>Enforce Puppetmaster Resource Restrictions: The Restoration Council and Military Chief actively restrict the crown's treasury, stipend, and military forces. Require the PCs to personally venture into the field or undertake hazardous covert missions to secure independent wealth, ancient relics, and unaligned allies.</p>\n<p>Emphasize Actionable Political Friction: Every regional crisis should offer multiple political levers. Resolving a crisis by force may alienate powerful Grandees, while negotiating with insurgents could provide leverage against the Restoration Council at the cost of provoking church zealots.</p>\n<p>Highlight Moral Ambiguity: Present factions with valid grievances and deep flaws. Republican insurgents fight against feudal tyranny but use terrorism; the Church of Nemeth opposes unnatural necromancy but seeks total religious autocracy; the Puppetmasters preserve order but murdered political rivals and exploit the dead.</p>\n<p>Maintain Player Agency: Treat the setting as a reactive engine. Allow player decisions to permanently shift the balance of power, potentially leading to the purge of the Restoration Council, the establishment of a constitutional monarchy, or total realm collapse.</p>\n",
      "gmSecrets": "The Restoration Council intentionally provoked the state bankruptcy to ensure the PCs cannot recruit an independent household guard without uncovering hidden wealth or relics.",
